# Final advice for Week 7

When you begin an object-oriented assignment, resist the temptation to immediately write `main()` and start coding.

First ask:

> **What are the important concepts in this problem, what responsibilities do they have, and how should they collaborate?**

Then design a small set of classes with clear responsibilities.

A good Week 7 solution is not the solution with the largest number of classes, the most inheritance, or the most advanced Java syntax. It is the solution in which the structure of the program makes sense.

If you can explain:

- why each class exists,
- what each class is responsible for,
- why one class contains another,
- why something is static or instance-based,
- and why you chose an enum or another type,

then you are developing one of the most important skills in software engineering: **reasoning about program design before and while writing code**.

**Inheritance** is an object-oriented programming mechanism that allows one class to derive characteristics and behavior from another class.

The existing class is typically called the **superclass**, **parent class**, or **base class**. The new class is called the **subclass**, **child class**, or **derived class**.

For example:

```java
public class Animal {
    private String name;

    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void eat() {
        System.out.println(name + " is eating.");
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
}
```

We could create a more specialized class:

```java
public class Dog extends Animal {

    public Dog(String name) {
        super(name);
    }

    public void bark() {
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }
}
```

Because `Dog` extends `Animal`, a `Dog` object can use the public methods defined by `Animal`:

```java
Dog dog = new Dog("Luna");

dog.eat();
dog.bark();
```

Conceptually:

```text
Animal
   ↑
   |
  Dog
```

A `Dog` **is an** `Animal`.

This "is-a" relationship is one of the most important ways to decide whether inheritance makes sense.

The keyword `extends` tells Java that one class inherits from another class.

```java
public class Dog extends Animal {
}
```

This means:

> `Dog` is a specialized type of `Animal`.

A `Dog` object contains the state and behavior associated with the `Animal` part of the object, plus anything defined specifically in `Dog`.

For example:

```java
public class Animal {

    public void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }
}
```

```java
public class Dog extends Animal {

    public void bark() {
        System.out.println("Barking");
    }
}
```

Then:

```java
Dog d = new Dog();

d.eat();   // inherited
d.bark();  // defined by Dog
```

A Java class can directly extend **only one class**.

This is called **single inheritance**.

The following is legal:

```java
class Dog extends Animal {
}
```

But Java does not allow:

```java
class Dog extends Animal, Pet {
}
```

A class can, however, implement multiple interfaces.

Not exactly.

A subclass receives access to much of the behavior of its superclass, but there are important restrictions.

Consider:

```java
public class Person {

    private String name;
    protected int age;
    public String country;

    public void speak() {
        System.out.println("Speaking");
    }
}
```

A subclass can directly access:

- `protected` members
- `public` members
- package-private members if it is in the same package

It **cannot directly access `private` fields or methods**.

For example:

```java
public class Student extends Person {

    public void printInfo() {
        System.out.println(age);      // allowed
        System.out.println(country);  // allowed

        // System.out.println(name);  // ERROR: name is private
    }
}
```

However, the `name` field still exists as part of the `Student` object. It simply cannot be accessed directly from the `Student` class.

If the superclass provides a public or protected method such as:

```java
public String getName() {
    return name;
}
```

then the subclass can use it:

```java
System.out.println(getName());
```

This preserves **encapsulation**.

This question depends somewhat on what we mean by "inherited."

Suppose we have:

```java
public class Person {

    private String name;

    public Person(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}
```

and:

```java
public class Student extends Person {

    public Student(String name) {
        super(name);
    }
}
```

A `Student` object still contains the state associated with `name`. The `Person` part of the object manages that field.

However, `Student` cannot write:

```java
name = "Alice";
```

because `name` is private to `Person`.

Instead, `Person` should provide appropriate methods for interacting with that state.

For example:

```java
public String getName() {
    return name;
}
```

The important design principle is:

> Inheritance does not eliminate encapsulation.

Subclasses should normally interact with superclass state through the superclass's methods rather than directly manipulating its fields.

**Method overriding** occurs when a subclass provides its own implementation of a method that already exists in its superclass.

Suppose:

```java
public class Animal {

    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Some animal sound");
    }
}
```

A `Dog` can provide a more specific implementation:

```java
public class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }
}
```

Now:

```java
Dog dog = new Dog();
dog.makeSound();
```

prints:

```text
Woof!
```

The subclass version replaces the inherited behavior for `Dog` objects.

`@Override` is an annotation that tells the compiler:

> I intend this method to override a method from a superclass or interface.

For example:

```java
@Override
public void makeSound() {
    System.out.println("Woof!");
}
```

Technically, Java can sometimes recognize an override without the annotation.

However, you should almost always use `@Override`.

It allows the compiler to detect mistakes.

Suppose the superclass contains:

```java
public void makeSound() {
}
```

but you accidentally write:

```java
@Override
public void makesSound() {
}
```

The compiler reports an error because `makesSound()` does not override anything.

Without `@Override`, Java might treat this as an entirely new method, making the mistake harder to detect.

Therefore:

```java
@Override
```

is not merely decoration. It is a useful correctness check.

These concepts sound similar but are very different.

## Overloading

**Method overloading** means having multiple methods with the same name but different parameter lists.

For example:

```java
public void print(int value) {
}

public void print(String value) {
}

public void print(int value, int count) {
}
```

These are three different methods.

Overloading normally happens within the same class, although inherited methods can also participate in overload resolution.

The compiler decides which overloaded method to call based primarily on the argument types.

---

## Overriding

**Method overriding** occurs when a subclass provides a new implementation of an inherited method.

```java
class Animal {

    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Animal sound");
    }
}
```

```java
class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Woof");
    }
}
```

The method name and parameter types must match the inherited method.

A useful summary is:

| Concept | What changes? | Typically decided when? |
|---|---|---|
| Overloading | Parameter list | Compile time |
| Overriding | Implementation in subclass | Runtime behavior can depend on object type |

The keyword `super` refers to the superclass portion of the current object.

It has several important uses.

## Calling a superclass constructor

```java
public class Student extends Person {

    public Student(String name) {
        super(name);
    }
}
```

Here:

```java
super(name);
```

calls the constructor of `Person`.

---

## Calling a superclass method

Suppose:

```java
class Animal {

    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Animal sound");
    }
}
```

A subclass can override the method while still using the superclass implementation:

```java
class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        super.makeSound();
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }
}
```

Calling:

```java
dog.makeSound();
```

would print:

```text
Animal sound
Woof!
```

When creating a subclass object, the superclass portion of the object must also be initialized.

Consider:

```java
public class Person {

    private String name;

    public Person(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}
```

Then:

```java
public class Student extends Person {

    private String major;

    public Student(String name, String major) {
        super(name);
        this.major = major;
    }
}
```

The statement:

```java
super(name);
```

allows the `Person` constructor to initialize the `Person` part of the object.

The `Student` constructor then initializes the `Student`-specific part.

You can think of construction as proceeding from the general part of the object toward the specialized part:

```text
Person constructor
        ↓
Student constructor
```

If a constructor does not explicitly call another constructor, Java automatically attempts to insert:

```java
super();
```

as its first statement.

For example:

```java
public Student() {
}
```

is approximately treated as:

```java
public Student() {
    super();
}
```

This works only if the superclass has an accessible no-argument constructor.

Suppose:

```java
public class Person {

    public Person(String name) {
    }
}
```

There is no:

```java
Person()
```

constructor.

Therefore this subclass constructor will fail:

```java
public Student() {
}
```

You must instead write something such as:

```java
public Student(String name) {
    super(name);
}
```

This is a very common source of compiler errors when students first learn inheritance.

**Polymorphism** means that the same interface or superclass reference can represent objects of different concrete types.

Consider:

```java
class Animal {

    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Animal sound");
    }
}
```

```java
class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Woof");
    }
}
```

```java
class Cat extends Animal {

    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Meow");
    }
}
```

We can write:

```java
Animal a1 = new Dog();
Animal a2 = new Cat();
```

Both variables have the reference type:

```java
Animal
```

but they refer to different types of objects.

Then:

```java
a1.makeSound();
a2.makeSound();
```

prints:

```text
Woof
Meow
```

The same method call:

```java
makeSound()
```

produces behavior appropriate for the actual object.

That is polymorphism.

Suppose we are writing a drawing program.

Without polymorphism, we might write:

```java
if (shapeType.equals("circle")) {
    // draw circle
} else if (shapeType.equals("rectangle")) {
    // draw rectangle
} else if (shapeType.equals("triangle")) {
    // draw triangle
}
```

This approach becomes difficult to maintain as new shapes are added.

Instead, we could define:

```java
public abstract class Shape {

    public abstract void draw();
}
```

Then:

```java
public class Circle extends Shape {

    @Override
    public void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing circle");
    }
}
```

```java
public class Rectangle extends Shape {

    @Override
    public void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing rectangle");
    }
}
```

Now we can write:

```java
Shape[] shapes = {
    new Circle(),
    new Rectangle(),
    new Circle()
};

for (Shape shape : shapes) {
    shape.draw();
}
```

The loop does not need to know what kind of shape each object is.

Each object knows how to perform its own version of:

```java
draw()
```

This reduces special-case logic and makes the program easier to extend.

An **abstract class** is a class that is intended to serve as a general superclass but is not intended to be instantiated directly.

It is declared with:

```java
abstract
```

For example:

```java
public abstract class Shape {

    private String color;

    public Shape(String color) {
        this.color = color;
    }

    public String getColor() {
        return color;
    }

    public abstract double area();
}
```

You cannot write:

```java
Shape shape = new Shape("red");
```

because `Shape` is abstract.

Instead, subclasses provide concrete implementations:

```java
public class Circle extends Shape {

    private double radius;

    public Circle(String color, double radius) {
        super(color);
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    @Override
    public double area() {
        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }
}
```

Then:

```java
Shape shape = new Circle("red", 5.0);
```

is valid.

An **abstract method** declares a method that must be implemented by concrete subclasses.

For example:

```java
public abstract double area();
```

Notice that there is no method body.

It ends with:

```java
;
```

rather than:

```java
{
}
```

A subclass must provide an implementation unless the subclass itself is also abstract.

For example:

```java
public class Rectangle extends Shape {

    private double width;
    private double height;

    public Rectangle(String color, double width, double height) {
        super(color);
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }

    @Override
    public double area() {
        return width * height;
    }
}
```

Abstract methods are useful when all subclasses should provide some behavior, but the superclass cannot provide one sensible implementation.

For example, every `Shape` has an area, but there is no single area formula that works for every shape.

Yes.

An abstract class can contain:

- fields
- constructors
- concrete methods
- abstract methods
- static methods

For example:

```java
public abstract class Employee {

    private String name;

    public Employee(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public abstract double calculatePay();
}
```

Here:

```java
getName()
```

is a regular concrete method.

```java
calculatePay()
```

is abstract.

Subclasses inherit the common behavior while implementing the specialized behavior.

Yes.

This often confuses students because an abstract class cannot itself be instantiated.

However, the constructor is still necessary to initialize the superclass portion of subclass objects.

For example:

```java
public abstract class Vehicle {

    private String manufacturer;

    public Vehicle(String manufacturer) {
        this.manufacturer = manufacturer;
    }
}
```

Then:

```java
public class Car extends Vehicle {

    public Car(String manufacturer) {
        super(manufacturer);
    }
}
```

Creating:

```java
new Car("Honda");
```

causes the `Vehicle` constructor to execute as part of constructing the `Car`.

An **interface** describes a contract that classes agree to follow.

For example:

```java
public interface Printable {

    void print();
}
```

A class can implement the interface:

```java
public class Report implements Printable {

    @Override
    public void print() {
        System.out.println("Printing report");
    }
}
```

Another completely different class can implement the same interface:

```java
public class Photo implements Printable {

    @Override
    public void print() {
        System.out.println("Printing photo");
    }
}
```

Now both objects can be treated as `Printable`:

```java
Printable p1 = new Report();
Printable p2 = new Photo();
```

This gives us polymorphism without requiring the classes to share the same superclass other than `Object`.

Yes.

This is one reason interfaces are so useful.

For example:

```java
public interface Printable {
    void print();
}
```

```java
public interface Saveable {
    void save();
}
```

A class can implement both:

```java
public class Report implements Printable, Saveable {

    @Override
    public void print() {
        System.out.println("Printing report");
    }

    @Override
    public void save() {
        System.out.println("Saving report");
    }
}
```

Java does not allow a class to directly extend multiple classes, but it does allow a class to implement multiple interfaces.

Yes.

Suppose:

```java
public interface Payable {

    double getPaymentAmount();
}
```

and:

```java
public class Invoice implements Payable {

    @Override
    public double getPaymentAmount() {
        return 250.0;
    }
}
```

Then:

```java
Payable p = new Invoice();
```

is valid.

This works for the same reason that:

```java
Animal a = new Dog();
```

works.

An `Invoice` **is a** `Payable` object because it implements the `Payable` contract.

You can then call:

```java
System.out.println(p.getPaymentAmount());
```

Polymorphism works with interfaces as well as classes.

The `instanceof` operator checks whether an object is compatible with a given type.

For example:

```java
if (animal instanceof Dog) {
    Dog dog = (Dog) animal;
    dog.fetch();
}
```

In modern Java, pattern matching can make this simpler:

```java
if (animal instanceof Dog dog) {
    dog.fetch();
}
```

This checks whether `animal` refers to a `Dog`.

If it does, `dog` becomes a safely typed reference to that object.

However, frequent use of `instanceof` may indicate that polymorphism could be used more effectively.

For example, this:

```java
if (animal instanceof Dog) {
    System.out.println("Woof");
} else if (animal instanceof Cat) {
    System.out.println("Meow");
}
```

is usually worse than:

```java
animal.makeSound();
```

with `Dog` and `Cat` overriding `makeSound()`.

Suppose we write:

```java
if (shape instanceof Circle) {
    // calculate circle area
} else if (shape instanceof Rectangle) {
    // calculate rectangle area
} else if (shape instanceof Triangle) {
    // calculate triangle area
}
```

Every time we add a new shape, this code must change.

Instead, each shape should generally know how to calculate its own area:

```java
public abstract class Shape {

    public abstract double area();
}
```

Then:

```java
double area = shape.area();
```

works regardless of the specific shape.

This approach moves behavior to the object that owns the relevant information.

It is often easier to:

- extend
- test
- understand
- maintain

That is one of the major motivations for polymorphism.

Composition is often preferable when one object uses or contains another object rather than being a specialized version of it.

