    while (true) {
        System.out.println(values[i]);
        i++;
    }
} catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
    // finished
}
```

The loop should instead express its condition directly:

```java
for (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(values[i]);
}
```

Exceptions should represent exceptional or erroneous conditions, not replace ordinary `if` statements and loops.

A `finally` block runs after a `try`/`catch` structure whether or not an exception occurred.

Example:

```java
try {
    // risky operation
} catch (IOException e) {
    // handle failure
} finally {
    System.out.println("Finished attempting operation");
}
```

Historically, `finally` was frequently used to close resources such as files.

Modern Java usually prefers **try-with-resources** for that purpose.

