# Part II: Files and input/output

A simple approach is:

```java
import java.io.File;
import java.io.FileNotFoundException;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class ReaderExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (Scanner input = new Scanner(new File("data.txt"))) {
            while (input.hasNextLine()) {
                String line = input.nextLine();
                System.out.println(line);
            }
        } catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
            System.out.println("Could not find data.txt");
        }
    }
}
```

The `try (...)` syntax is called **try-with-resources**.

It automatically closes the `Scanner` after the block finishes.

Try-with-resources automatically closes objects that implement `AutoCloseable`.

Example:

```java
try (Scanner input = new Scanner(new File("data.txt"))) {
    // use input
}
```

When the block ends, Java automatically closes `input`, even if an exception occurs.

This is generally preferable to manually calling:

```java
input.close();
```

inside complicated error-handling code.

One common approach uses `PrintWriter`.

```java
import java.io.PrintWriter;
import java.io.FileNotFoundException;

public class WriterExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (PrintWriter output = new PrintWriter("results.txt")) {
            output.println("Alice,95");
            output.println("Bob,87");
        } catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
            System.out.println("Could not create the output file.");
        }
    }
}
```

When the `PrintWriter` closes, buffered output is flushed to the file.

