Recursion is particularly natural when objects are linked recursively.

Later, when studying linked lists and trees, you may encounter structures such as:

```java
class Node {
    int value;
    Node next;
}
```

A recursive method could process the list:

```java
public static int sum(Node node) {
    if (node == null) {
        return 0;
    }

    return node.value + sum(node.next);
}
```

Here the data structure itself is recursive:

- a node contains a value,
- and a reference to the remainder of the list.

Trees are even more naturally recursive because each node may refer to smaller subtrees.

A tree is recursively structured.

A binary tree node might contain:

```java
class TreeNode {
    int value;
    TreeNode left;
    TreeNode right;
}
```

The left child is itself the root of a smaller tree.

The right child is also the root of a smaller tree.

That means many tree problems naturally have the form:

1. Solve the problem for the current node.
2. Recursively solve it for the left subtree.
3. Recursively solve it for the right subtree.

For example, counting nodes:

```java
public static int countNodes(TreeNode node) {
    if (node == null) {
        return 0;
    }

    return 1
        + countNodes(node.left)
        + countNodes(node.right);
}
```

This is one reason recursion becomes increasingly important when you begin studying data structures.

A public method may have a clean interface, while recursion needs additional state.

