**Linear search** examines elements one at a time until it finds the target or reaches the end.

Example:

```java
public static int linearSearch(int[] data, int target) {
    for (int i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
        if (data[i] == target) {
            return i;
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
```

If the target is found, the method returns its index.

If not, it returns `-1`.

For an array of size `n`:

- Best case: `O(1)`
- Worst case: `O(n)`
- Typical overall classification: `O(n)`

Linear search works whether or not the data is sorted.

**Binary search** searches a **sorted** collection by repeatedly comparing the target with the middle element.

If the target is smaller than the middle element, binary search continues in the left half.

If the target is larger, it continues in the right half.

If the values are equal, the target has been found.

Example:

```java
public static int binarySearch(int[] data, int target) {
    int low = 0;
    int high = data.length - 1;

    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

        if (data[mid] == target) {
            return mid;
        } else if (target < data[mid]) {
            high = mid - 1;
        } else {
            low = mid + 1;
        }
    }

    return -1;
}
```

Its worst-case time complexity is:

```text
O(log n)
```

**Selection sort** repeatedly finds the smallest remaining value and places it into its correct position.

Suppose we begin with:

```text
[7, 3, 9, 2]
```

The algorithm finds the smallest value, `2`, and swaps it into the first position:

```text
[2, 3, 9, 7]
```

Then it searches the remaining unsorted portion:

```text
[3, 9, 7]
```

The smallest is already `3`.

Then:

```text
[9, 7]
```

The smallest is `7`, producing:

```text
[2, 3, 7, 9]
```

A Java implementation:

```java
public static void selectionSort(int[] data) {
    for (int i = 0; i < data.length - 1; i++) {
        int minIndex = i;

        for (int j = i + 1; j < data.length; j++) {
            if (data[j] < data[minIndex]) {
                minIndex = j;
            }
        }

        int temp = data[i];
        data[i] = data[minIndex];
        data[minIndex] = temp;
    }
}
```

Its time complexity is:

```text
O(n^2)
```

