# Final perspective

The most important lesson from Week 11 is not memorizing a table of Big-O values.

Instead, learn to ask:

> **How much more work does this algorithm perform when the input becomes much larger?**

Searching and sorting provide concrete examples of why this question matters.

A linear search and binary search may both appear instantaneous on an array of 20 values. On millions or billions of values, however, their different growth rates become extremely important.

Likewise, selection sort is easy to understand and implement, but its quadratic growth makes it poorly suited to very large collections. Studying these simple algorithms builds the reasoning skills needed to understand more advanced algorithms and data structures later in the computer science curriculum.

A **dynamic data structure** is a structure whose size can change while the program is running.

An ordinary Java array has a fixed length after it is created:

```java
int[] values = new int[10];
```

That array always has room for exactly 10 `int` values. You can change the values stored in it, but you cannot change the array's length.

By contrast, a dynamic structure such as an `ArrayList` or linked list can grow and shrink as elements are added or removed.

```java
ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

names.add("Ada");
names.add("Grace");
names.add("Alan");
names.remove("Grace");
```

The number of logical elements in `names` changes over time.

The word **dynamic** does not mean that the data structure has unlimited memory. It still uses memory supplied by the computer. It means that your program does not have to choose one fixed size in advance.

A **node** is a small object used as a building block for a linked data structure.

A simple node class might be:

```java
public class Node {
    String data;
    Node next;

    public Node(String data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.next = null;
    }
}
```

Notice something unusual:

```java
Node next;
```

A `Node` contains a reference to another `Node`.

That is perfectly legal in Java. The field does not contain the entire second node inside the first node. It contains only a reference to another object.

A more general version might use generics:

```java
public class Node<T> {
    T data;
    Node<T> next;

    public Node(T data) {
        this.data = data;
    }
}
```

When traversing a linked list, we usually want to move through the nodes without changing the actual `head` reference.

For example:

```java
Node current = head;

while (current != null) {
    System.out.println(current.data);
    current = current.next;
}
```

Here:

```java
current = current.next;
```

moves the temporary reference forward.

The list itself is unchanged.

If instead you wrote:

```java
while (head != null) {
    System.out.println(head.data);
    head = head.next;
}
```

you would repeatedly change `head`.

When the loop ended, `head` would be `null`, and the object storing the list might lose access to all its nodes.

The general principle is:

> Use a temporary reference for traversal unless you intentionally want to modify the structure.

A singly linked list generally requires sequential searching.

Example:

```java
public boolean contains(String target) {
    Node current = head;

    while (current != null) {
        if (current.data.equals(target)) {
            return true;
        }

        current = current.next;
    }

    return false;
}
```

In the worst case, the algorithm checks every node.

Therefore the running time is:

```text
O(n)
```

This differs from indexed access in an array or `ArrayList`, where accessing a known index is normally:

```text
O(1)
```

**Traversal** means visiting nodes without changing the structure.

Example:

```java
Node current = head;

while (current != null) {
    System.out.println(current.data);
    current = current.next;
}
```

**Modification** means changing references or data so that the structure itself changes.

Example:

```java
newNode.next = head;
head = newNode;
```

Traversal bugs usually cause incorrect processing or infinite loops.

Modification bugs can be more severe because they can disconnect nodes, create cycles, or corrupt the list.

It is useful to distinguish between code that only reads structure and code that changes structure.

A generic linked list can store different object types while preserving type safety.

Instead of:

```java
class Node {
    Object data;
    Node next;
}
```

we can write:

```java
class Node<T> {
    T data;
    Node<T> next;

    Node(T data) {
        this.data = data;
    }
}
```

Then:

```java
Node<String> nameNode = new Node<>("Ada");
Node<Integer> numberNode = new Node<>(42);
```

The type parameter `T` represents the element type.

Generics prevent many type-casting errors and are a major part of Java's collections framework.

Yes, and in Java this is very common.

For example:

```java
class Student {
    private String name;
    private int id;
}
```

A node could store a `Student`:

```java
class Node {
    Student data;
    Node next;
}
```

Or generically:

```java
Node<Student>
```

For primitive-like values, wrapper classes are used:

```java
Node<Integer>
Node<Double>
```

This parallels the use of generics in the Java Collections Framework.

