A **node** is the basic building block of a tree.

A binary-tree node might be represented in Java as:

```java
class TreeNode {
    int value;
    TreeNode left;
    TreeNode right;

    TreeNode(int value) {
        this.value = value;
        this.left = null;
        this.right = null;
    }
}
```

Each node contains:

1. A value
2. A reference to the left child
3. A reference to the right child

The references may be `null` if the corresponding child does not exist.

A typical iterative Java method looks like:

```java
public boolean contains(int target) {
    TreeNode current = root;

    while (current != null) {
        if (target == current.value) {
            return true;
        } else if (target < current.value) {
            current = current.left;
        } else {
            current = current.right;
        }
    }

    return false;
}
```

At each step, the ordering property tells us which direction to follow.

Yes.

```java
private boolean contains(TreeNode node, int target) {
    if (node == null) {
        return false;
    }

    if (target == node.value) {
        return true;
    }

    if (target < node.value) {
        return contains(node.left, target);
    } else {
        return contains(node.right, target);
    }
}
```

The recursion naturally follows the recursive structure of a tree.

One common implementation is:

```java
public void insert(int value) {
    root = insert(root, value);
}

private TreeNode insert(TreeNode node, int value) {
    if (node == null) {
        return new TreeNode(value);
    }

    if (value < node.value) {
        node.left = insert(node.left, value);
    } else if (value > node.value) {
        node.right = insert(node.right, value);
    }

    return node;
}
```

The important idea is that the helper method returns the root of the updated subtree.

For a binary tree, inorder traversal follows:

1. Traverse the left subtree.
2. Visit the current node.
3. Traverse the right subtree.

Mnemonic:

```text
LEFT - NODE - RIGHT
```

Java:

```java
private void inorder(TreeNode node) {
    if (node == null) {
        return;
    }

    inorder(node.left);
    System.out.println(node.value);
    inorder(node.right);
}
```

Preorder traversal follows:

1. Visit the current node.
2. Traverse the left subtree.
3. Traverse the right subtree.

Mnemonic:

```text
NODE - LEFT - RIGHT
```

Java:

```java
private void preorder(TreeNode node) {
    if (node == null) {
        return;
    }

    System.out.println(node.value);
    preorder(node.left);
    preorder(node.right);
}
```

Postorder traversal follows:

1. Traverse the left subtree.
2. Traverse the right subtree.
3. Visit the current node.

Mnemonic:

```text
LEFT - RIGHT - NODE
```

Java:

```java
private void postorder(TreeNode node) {
    if (node == null) {
        return;
    }

    postorder(node.left);
    postorder(node.right);
    System.out.println(node.value);
}
```

Usually:

```java
if (node == null)
```

This represents an empty subtree.

For example:

```java
private int countNodes(TreeNode node) {
    if (node == null) {
        return 0;
    }

    return 1 + countNodes(node.left)
             + countNodes(node.right);
}
```

The empty tree contains zero nodes.

```java
private int countNodes(TreeNode node) {
    if (node == null) {
        return 0;
    }

    return 1
         + countNodes(node.left)
         + countNodes(node.right);
}
```

The logic is:

```text
number of nodes =
    1 for the current node
    + nodes in left subtree
    + nodes in right subtree
```

This is a very important recursive pattern.

```java
private int countLeaves(TreeNode node) {
    if (node == null) {
        return 0;
    }

    if (node.left == null && node.right == null) {
        return 1;
    }

    return countLeaves(node.left)
         + countLeaves(node.right);
}
```

A node is a leaf only when both child references are `null`.

Using the convention that an empty tree has height `-1` and a leaf has height `0`:

```java
private int height(TreeNode node) {
    if (node == null) {
        return -1;
    }

    int leftHeight = height(node.left);
    int rightHeight = height(node.right);

    return 1 + Math.max(leftHeight, rightHeight);
}
```

The tree's height is one more than the larger height of its two subtrees.

If your course defines height differently, the base value may change.

It means the node has no left child.

For example:

```java
if (node.left == null) {
    System.out.println("No left child");
}
```

The reference does not point to any node object.

Look at where the current node is processed.

### Preorder

```java
process(node);
visit(node.left);
visit(node.right);
```

### Inorder

```java
visit(node.left);
process(node);
visit(node.right);
```

### Postorder

```java
visit(node.left);
visit(node.right);
process(node);
```

The position of `process(node)` determines the traversal.

